  "historical_output_text": " The monotonuous and repetitive rain during a wet spring, known as “showers in springtime”, has two key factors causing the effect of wet conditions.\nFirst of these, and most obviously present in our experience as a society of living on Earth is our planet orbit, tilted and rotating at different inclines, which results in our weather patterns and temperature. This is the main driver that makes seasons.\nSecond of course the sun plays a big role by making the temperature increase, melting the snow or causing it fall and by warming water, making evocat and condencing in clouds. As a side note it's interesting that clouds form in cold air when moisture from warmer, less dense areas moves upwards into cold air. So in this case, when it is warmer, more moist conditions we have a good chance at cloud cover or a rain cloud forming",
